2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,6,6,6,6,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,6,6,6,6,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,6,6,6,6,8,2,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[2,2,2,2,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,3,3,3,3,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0,0,8,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**Reading the structure.** Every grid is a lattice of equal rectangular cells separated by full lines of 8. So I treat each block of non-8 rows and non-8 columns as one \"cell\", and give each cell a (row band, column band) index. A few cells contain a single coloured marker pixel, always colour 2 or colour 3.\n\n**What the outputs do.** Take any two markers of the same colour that sit in the same cell row or the same cell column. Every cell between them, endpoints included, is filled entirely with that colour. So markers that line up get joined by a bar of cells.\n\nI checked this against each pair:\n\n- **Pair 1**\n  - The 2-markers sit at cells (0,3), (4,6), (6,3) and (6,6).\n  - This gives three bars: column 3 from row 0 to row 6, column 6 from row 4 to row 6, and row 6 from column 3 to column 6.\n  - The two 3-markers at (1,1) and (1,6) give a bar along row 1.\n  - That 3-bar crosses the 2-bar at cell (1,3), and in the output that cell is colour 6.\n- **Pair 2**\n  - The 2-markers give a vertical bar in column 1, and the 3-markers give a horizontal bar in row 2.\n  - The crossing cell (2,1) is colour 6.\n- **Pair 3**\n  - The 2-markers produce a horizontal bar and the 3-markers produce a horizontal bar.\n  - The two bars do not cross, so no 6 appears.\n\nSo the rule has one extra part: a cell covered by bars of two different colours becomes 6.\n\n**Test input.**\n- The 3-markers are at cells (0,2) and (4,2), so column 2 is filled with 3 from cell row 0 to row 4.\n- The 2-markers are at (1,0), (1,4) and (3,0).\n  - (1,0)–(1,4) gives a horizontal bar along cell row 1.\n  - (1,0)–(3,0) gives a vertical bar down cell column 0.\n- The two colours cross at cell (1,2), which becomes 6.\n\n**Construction stages.**\n1. Draw each colour's bars, in increasing colour order, and emit a canvas after each colour.\n2. Recolour the crossing cells to 6 and emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    def bands(is_div):\n        res, cur = [], []\n        for i, d in enumerate(is_div):\n            if d:\n                if cur: res.append((cur[0], cur[-1])); cur = []\n            else:\n                cur.append(i)\n        if cur: res.append((cur[0], cur[-1]))\n        return res\n\n    rb = bands([all(g[y, :] == 8) for y in range(H)])\n    cb = bands([all(g[:, x] == 8) for x in range(W)])\n\n    def band_of(v, bs):\n        for i, (a, b) in enumerate(bs):\n            if a <= v <= b:\n                return i\n        return None\n\n    markers = {}\n    for y in range(H):\n        for x in range(W):\n            c = int(g[y, x])\n            if c not in (0, 8):\n                markers.setdefault(c, []).append((band_of(y, rb), band_of(x, cb)))\n\n    cover = {}  # cell -> set of colours\n    for c in sorted(markers):\n        ms = markers[c]\n        for i in range(len(ms)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(ms)):\n                (r1, c1), (r2, c2) = ms[i], ms[j]\n                cells = []\n                if r1 == r2:\n                    cells = [(r1, k) for k in range(min(c1, c2), max(c1, c2) + 1)]\n                elif c1 == c2:\n                    cells = [(k, c1) for k in range(min(r1, r2), max(r1, r2) + 1)]\n                for (r, k) in cells:\n                    cover.setdefault((r, k), set()).add(c)\n                    y0, y1 = rb[r]; x0, x1 = cb[k]\n                    out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Filled cells between aligned colour-{c} markers with colour {c}.\")\n\n    changed = False\n    for (r, k), cs in cover.items():\n        if len(cs) > 1:\n            y0, y1 = rb[r]; x0, x1 = cb[k]\n            out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = 6\n            changed = True\n    if changed:\n        emit(out, \"Cells where bars of two colours cross become colour 6.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Recolour the crossing cells to 6 and emit the final grid."}],"movement":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Filled cells between aligned colour-2 markers with colour 2."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Filled cells between aligned colour-3 markers with colour 3."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every cell between them, endpoints included, is filled entirely with that colour."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0db27f0e181d5dbfed7b61371d8c03001939f4398d51cca4d2a82c49aa779d9e","task_sha256":"ecaba967ecdc510816ce704da3eb0dae11915ce38ad1110f24c4fe5f30fb4e9e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
